															<div id=3D"tab-opinion-5139454" class=3D"">
						<div class=3D"-display-inline-block text-left">
														=09

															SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES<p></p>

															JEFFREY CLYDE PITTS <em>v.</em> MISSISSIPPI<p></p>

															on petition for writ of certiorari to the
supreme court of mississippi<p></p>

															No. 24=E2=80=931159.=E2=80=83Decided November 24, 2025<p></p=
>

															<p></p>

															Per Curiam.<p></p>

															Ordinarily, the Sixth Amendment=E2=80=99s Confrontation
Clause =E2=80=9Cguarantees the defendant a face-to-face meeting with
witnesses appearing before the trier of fact.=E2=80=9D <em>Coy</em> v.
<em>Iowa</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"https://supreme.justia.com=
/cases/federal/us/487/1012/">487 U.S.
1012</a>, 1016 (1988). In child-abuse cases, however, that rule
sometimes gives way. Consistent with the Sixth Amendment, a court
may screen a child witness from the defendant when =E2=80=9Cnecessary to
protect [the child] from trauma that would be caused by testifying
in the physical presence of the defendant, at least where such
trauma would impair the child=E2=80=99s ability to communicate.=E2=80=9D
<em>Maryland</em> v. <em>Craig</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"http=
s://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/497/836/">497 U.S.
836</a>, 857 (1990).<p></p>

															Still, before invoking this procedure, a court
must proceed with care. It must =E2=80=9Chear evidence=E2=80=9D and make a
=E2=80=9Ccase-specific=E2=80=9D finding of =E2=80=9C[t]he requisite .&nbsp;=
.&nbsp;.
necessity.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 855. Simply pointing to a state statut=
e
that authorizes screening, even one premised on =E2=80=9Cgeneralized
finding[s]=E2=80=9D of necessity, will not suffice. <em>Coy</em>, 487
U.&nbsp;S., at 1021. Because the Mississippi Supreme Court departed
from these principles, we reverse.<p></p>

															I<p></p>

															In May 2020, A.&nbsp;G.&nbsp;C. spent a
weekend visiting her father, Jeffrey Pitts. After returning home,
A.&nbsp;G.&nbsp;C. told her mother that she had been sexually
abused. Eventually, that report led to criminal charges against
Pitts.<p></p>

															At trial, the State moved for permission to
place a screen between A.&nbsp;G.&nbsp;C. and Pitts when she took
the witness stand. In support of its motion, the State pointed to a
Mississippi statute providing that child witnesses =E2=80=9Cshall have the
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. righ[t]=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9Ca properly constructed screen =
that
would permit the judge and jury in the courtroom .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. to
see the child but would obscure the child=E2=80=99s view of the defendant.=
=E2=80=9D
Miss. Code Ann. =C2=A799=E2=80=9343=E2=80=93101(2)(g) (2020).<p></p>

															Pitts objected. He did not question the
statute=E2=80=99s mandatory terms. But, he said, those terms had to give
way to the Sixth Amendment=E2=80=99s demands. 405 So. 3d 20, 31 (Miss. App.
2023). And, he submitted, the State had not attempted to meet, and
could not meet, its Sixth Amendment burden of showing that
screening was necessary in the particular circumstances of his
case. 405 So. 3d 1238, 1243 (Miss. 2025).<p></p>

															The trial judge granted the State=E2=80=99s motion. In
doing so, the judge reasoned that the =E2=80=9Cstatute .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.
appears to be mandatory,=E2=80=9D and expressed =E2=80=9Cconcerns about [hi=
s]
ability to declare the statute unconstitutional and fail to follow
it.=E2=80=9D App. to Pet. for Cert. 36a (App.).<p></p>

															After a jury convicted him, Pitts appealed. 405
So. 3d, at 31. Invoking <em>Coy</em> and <em>Craig</em>, he argued that
the trial court had failed to make the case-specific finding of
necessity the Sixth Amendment requires and, as remedy, sought a new
trial. 405 So. 3d, at 31=E2=80=9335.<p></p>

															Ultimately, a divided Mississippi Supreme Court
rejected Pitts=E2=80=99s arguments. The court did not dispute that the
trial court failed to make a case-specific finding of necessity.
See 405 So. 3d, at 1246. Instead, the court sought to distinguish
<em>Coy</em> and <em>Craig</em> on various grounds. 405 So. 3d, at
1248=E2=80=931252. With those distinctions in hand, the court then
proceeded to hold that Mississippi=E2=80=99s mandatory statute provided
sufficient authority for the screening in this case. <em>Id</em>., at
1254=E2=80=931255. Unpersuaded, a dissent argued that <em>Coy</em> and
<em>Craig</em> controlled this case and that the trial court failed
to comply with their terms. 405 So. 3d, at 1255 (King, J.,
dissenting).<p></p>

															After the Mississippi Supreme Court ruled, Pitts
sought certiorari.<p></p>

															II<p></p>

															Under <em>Coy</em> and <em>Craig</em>, a trial
court may not deny a defendant his Sixth Amendment right to meet
his accusers face to face simply because a state statute permits
screening. Nor may a court authorize screening based on
=E2=80=9Cgeneralized finding[s]=E2=80=9D of necessity underlying such a sta=
tute.
<em>Coy</em>, 487 U.&nbsp;S., at 1021. Instead, the Sixth Amendment
tolerates screening in child-abuse cases only if a court =E2=80=9Chear[s]
evidence=E2=80=9D and issues a =E2=80=9Ccase-specific=E2=80=9D finding of =
=E2=80=9C[t]he requisite
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. necessity.=E2=80=9D See <em>Craig</em>, 497 U.&nbsp;S., at
855. The Mississippi Supreme Court attempted to avoid these
constraints by distinguishing <em>Coy</em> and <em>Craig</em> in
various ways. But none of the court=E2=80=99s distinctions persuades.
Consider the most salient theories it pressed:<p></p>

															First, the court pointed to a victims=E2=80=99 rights
provision in Mississippi=E2=80=99s State Constitution. 405 So. 3d, at
1246=E2=80=931249. That provision affords the state legislature the power
=E2=80=9Cto enact .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. laws to .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. protect the
rights guaranteed to victims.=E2=80=9D Art. 3, =C2=A726A(3). And, the court
observed, the state legislature exercised this power when it
adopted the mandatory screening law the trial court applied in this
case. 405 So. 3d, at 1249. But, true as all that may be, it is also
irrelevant. When state law conflicts with the Federal Constitution,
the latter controls. Art. VI, cl.&nbsp;2. And under the Sixth
Amendment, neither state screening statutes, nor the =E2=80=9Cgeneralized
finding[s]=E2=80=9D on which they are premised, are enough to overcome a
defendant=E2=80=99s right to face-to-face confrontation. <em>Coy</em>, 487
U.&nbsp;S., at 1021.<p></p>

															Second, the court stressed that Mississippi=E2=80=99s
statute mandates screening in child-abuse cases while the Iowa
statute in <em>Coy</em> only afforded trial courts discretion to
screen. 405 So. 3d, at 1249=E2=80=931250. But, if anything, the fact that
Mississippi=E2=80=99s statute is mandatory=E2=80=94and thus <em>never</em> =
requires a
case-specific finding of necessity=E2=80=94renders it =E2=80=9Cmore
constitutionally problematic than the statute at issue in
<em>Coy</em>, not less so.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 1258 (King, J.,
dissenting).<p></p>

															Third, the court emphasized that
A.&nbsp;G.&nbsp;C. was four years old at the time of trial.
<em>Id</em>., at 1250 (majority opinion). But <em>Craig</em> involved a
6-year-old witness. 497 U.&nbsp;S., at 840. And though a witness=E2=80=99s
age is a relevant consideration, <em>Craig</em> made plain that a
court must =E2=80=9Chear evidence=E2=80=9D and make a =E2=80=9Ccase-specifi=
c=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cfinding of
necessity=E2=80=9D before denying a defendant the right to face-to-face
confrontation in a child-abuse case. See <em>id</em>., at 855.<p></p>

															Fourth, the court observed that in <em>Coy</em>
the government and the defendant disputed who committed the alleged
assault, while in this case the identity of the alleged perpetrator
was not in question. 405 So. 3d, at 1250. But the Sixth Amendment
right to confront one=E2=80=99s accusers face to face does not only apply
in cases where identity is at issue. See <em>Craig</em>, 497
U.&nbsp;S., at 840 (involving a known perpetrator). Nor does
<em>Craig</em>=E2=80=99s exception for child-abuse cases automatically appl=
y
just because identity happens to be uncontested. See <em>id</em>., at
855=E2=80=93856.<p></p>

															Finally, the court noted that the child witness
and lawyers in <em>Craig</em> were placed in a different room from
the defendant, with cross-examination conducted over closed-circuit
television. 405 So. 3d, at 1251. In this case, by contrast,
everyone remained in the courtroom, the witness and defendant
separated only by a screen. <em>Id</em>., at 1244, 1251. But both
approaches deviate from the Sixth Amendment=E2=80=99s usual rule that a
defendant is entitled to meet his accusers =E2=80=9Cface to face.=E2=80=9D =
See
<em>Coy</em>, 487 U.&nbsp;S., at 1016; <em>Craig</em>, 497 U.&nbsp;S.,
at 844. And both thus require a case-specific finding of necessity.
<em>Id.</em>, at 855.<p></p>

															Before this Court, the State does not so much
defend the Mississippi Supreme Court=E2=80=99s various efforts to
distinguish <em>Coy</em> and <em>Craig</em> as press a different
argument still. As the State sees it, the trial court <em>did</em>
=E2=80=9Chear evidence=E2=80=9D and make a =E2=80=9Ccase-specific=E2=80=9D =
=E2=80=9Cfinding of necessity.=E2=80=9D
See <em>Craig</em>, 497 U.&nbsp;S., at 855<em>.</em><p></p>

															We disagree. At trial, to be sure, the
prosecution represented that A.&nbsp;G.&nbsp;C.=E2=80=99s guardian believed
it would be difficult for her to testify face to face with her
father. App. 6a. But the prosecution expressly rejected the notion
that it had =E2=80=9Cto put on any proof,=E2=80=9D choosing to rely instead=
 on
Mississippi=E2=80=99s mandatory =E2=80=9Cright=E2=80=9D to screening. <em>I=
d</em>., at 6a=E2=80=937a.
And the trial judge proceeded to rule that the =E2=80=9Cstatute
.&nbsp;.&nbsp;. appears to be mandatory=E2=80=9D and expressed concerns
about =E2=80=9Cfail[ing] to follow it.=E2=80=9D <em>Id</em>., at 36a. Those=
 arguments
and conclusions fall well short of the procedures and findings
<em>Coy</em> and <em>Craig</em> require.<p></p>

															III<p></p>

															Having resolved that much, we pause to
underscore what we leave unresolved. Just because a constitutional
error took place at trial does not necessarily mean a new one must
be held. Even constitutional errors are sometimes subject to a
=E2=80=9Charmless-error=E2=80=9D rule and do not require a new trial if the
prosecution can show =E2=80=9Cbeyond a reasonable doubt that the error
complained of did not contribute to the verdict obtained.=E2=80=9D
<em>Chapman</em> v. <em>California</em>, <a class=3D"related-case" href=3D"=
https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/386/18/">386 U.S.
18</a>, 23=E2=80=9324 (1967). This Court has held that the denial of the
right to face-to-face confrontation is among those errors =E2=80=9Csubject
to that harmless-error analysis.=E2=80=9D <em>Coy</em>, 487 U.&nbsp;S., at
1021. Accordingly, on remand the State remains free to argue, and
the Mississippi Supreme Court remains free to consider, whether the
error in this case warrants a new trial under the harmless-error
standard.<p></p>

															The petition for certiorari is granted, the
judgment of the Mississippi Supreme Court is reversed, and the case
is remanded for further proceedings not inconsistent with this
opinion.<p></p>

															<em>It is so ordered.</em><p></p>

													</div>
					</div>
